//! ui/cycle_mitigation — the ownership invariant, checkable; and the
//! structural diff between two trees.
//! 
//! Status: first-party library v0.1.0 (native tier). Pure `.nv`: no
//! builtins beyond `print`/`println`/`assert`, so it runs on `run` today
//! and stays portable to `run-vm`/`build` later.
//! 
//! ## What this module is for
//! 
//! `docs/MEMORY_MODEL.md` §4 and `docs/UI_SPEC.md` §3 both record the
//! reference-cycle problem as **Open**, with four candidate strategies
//! and candidate 3 ("parent strongly owns child, child holds an
//! `unowned` parent back-reference, enforced by the framework") as the
//! leading one. The original version of this module took that candidate
//! literally and shipped three functions whose bodies were all `()` or
//! `true`.
//! 
//! It could not do otherwise. The Noctivue runtime that exists today is a
//! tree-walking interpreter over **values**: a widget is copied when it
//! is stored, there is no heap identity, no reference counting and no
//! `weak`/`unowned` annotation in the grammar. So there is no cycle to
//! mitigate, and a `unowned parent: Widget` field would be a *copy* of
//! the parent — a second, silently stale copy of the whole subtree, not
//! a back-reference.
//! 
//! Rather than ship three stubs, this module keeps the **invariant** and
//! drops the mechanism, and it can do that because the invariant has a
//! checkable form:
//! 
//! - **Acyclicity by rank function.** Every node carries `depth`; a
//!   child's depth is its parent's + 1; a root's is 0. A value that
//!   contained itself would be its own ancestor at a strictly smaller
//!   depth, which is impossible. [`validate_tree`] re-derives every depth
//!   from the root and rejects the first disagreement — so "no cycles" is
//!   a *proved* property of a tree that passes, not an assertion about
//!   a runtime that is not there yet.
//! - **One owner per node.** A non-zero `uid` must be unique among a
//!   node's children, so no node is owned twice. Checked per sibling
//!   list, which is the level at which double ownership can arise; a
//!   global check would need a set, and the language cannot build one.
//! - **Shape.** A leaf takes no children, a single-child wrapper takes
//!   exactly one, a container takes zero or more.
//! 
//! What is *not* claimed: that this is the strategy Noctivue will adopt.
//! When a managed mode with real ARC lands, this module is the place
//! that has to be revisited, because its checks are checks on values and
//! a heap object needs different ones. That is stated in
//! `docs/overview.md` too, and it is why nothing here is named as if the
//! Open question were settled.
//! 
//! ## The diff
//! 
//! [`diff_widgets`] walks two trees and reports what changed: which node,
//! at which index, under which parent, and in what way. It exists because
//! the original module's `diff_props` returned
//! `Map<String, Value>` and built a mutation list with `push` — a `Map`
//! does not exist in the language and a list cannot be grown, so
//! neither half of that was writable. Both are replaced by
//! fixed-size values ([`Mutation`], [`Diff`]) and a chain of `Mutation`s
//! threaded through the recursion, which costs nothing and has no cap.
//! 
//! Children are paired **positionally**, with one exception: when a node
//! carries a non-zero `uid` and the two sides disagree on it, the old
//! child is looked up by `uid` in the new child list, and a hit is
//! reported as [`MoveChild`] rather than as a spurious replace. That is
//! a keyed diff for the one case where keying is unambiguous, and it is
//! honest about being partial: a full keyed diff needs an LCS, and this
//! language has no way to hold one.
//! 
//! The walk is bounded by depth rather than by the interpreter's call
//! stack: [`diff_widgets`] recurses to [`DIFF_MAX_DEPTH`] and stops.
//! Past that depth the node's own properties are still compared and its
//! children are not descended into, which is stated rather than hidden.

import widget
import widget::Widget
import widget::WidgetKind

/// How deep [`diff_widgets`] descends. Deeper subtrees are compared at
/// the node level only.
/// 
/// A bound, not an accident: the tree-walking interpreter's call-stack
/// budget is small, and an unbounded recursive walk over a deep tree
/// overflows it with no diagnostic at all. Eight levels is far past any
/// hand-written UI tree, and [`diff_widgets_depth`] is there for a caller
/// who wants a different bound.
export fn diff_max_depth() -> Int:
    8

/// What changed about a node. Unit variants, as everywhere in this
/// package: a payload variant would have to carry the `Widget`, and a
/// payload variant cannot be constructed in this language.
export enum MutationKind:
    ReplaceNode()
    Reidentify()
    Retitle()
    Enable()
    Disable()
    Repad()
    Realign()
    InsertChild()
    RemoveChild()
    MoveChild()

/// One change, located.
/// 
/// - `parent_uid` is the `uid` of the node the change happens under, and
///   `0` when the change is at the root or the parent is unidentified.
/// - `index` is the child slot the change applies to; `-1` for a change
///   to the node itself rather than to one of its children.
/// - `uid` is the affected node's own `uid`, or the `uid` a moved node
///   carries, and `0` when it has none.
/// - `detail` is a one-line human-readable rendering, so a report can be
///   printed without a second switch over [`MutationKind`] that would
///   have to be kept in step with this one.
/// - `next` chains to the next change; the chain is what makes an
///   unbounded mutation list possible in a language with no list growth.
export struct Mutation:
    kind: MutationKind
    parent_uid: Int
    index: Int
    uid: Int
    detail: String
    next: Option<Mutation>

/// The result of a diff: how many changes, and the chain of them.
/// 
/// There is deliberately no `overflow` flag and no capacity: the chain is
/// a linked list, so the count is exact and there is no limit to
/// document.
export struct Diff:
    n: Int
    head: Option<Mutation>

/// INTERNAL — a worklist node for [`validate_tree`]. The validator walks
/// a tree with a worklist rather than by recursion so that tree depth
/// costs heap-free `Option` chaining instead of interpreter call frames;
/// a deep tree must not be able to overflow the stack just by being
/// validated.
export struct Pending:
    node: Widget
    next: Option<Pending>

/// INTERNAL — a fresh mutation, chained to `rest`. Every mutation in
/// this module is built here, so the six fields cannot be filled in the
/// wrong order anywhere.
export fn mutation(k: MutationKind, parent_uid: Int, index: Int, uid: Int, detail: String, rest: Option<Mutation>) -> Mutation:
    Mutation { kind: k, parent_uid: parent_uid, index: index, uid: uid, detail: detail, next: rest }

/// INTERNAL — [`mutation`] with nothing after it, for the leaf cases.
export fn lone(k: MutationKind, parent_uid: Int, index: Int, uid: Int, detail: String) -> Mutation:
    mutation(k, parent_uid, index, uid, detail, None)

/// INTERNAL — `head` followed by `tail`, i.e. the chain `head` then
/// everything in `tail`. Walks `head` and rebuilds it, because there is
/// no pointer mutation; the reverse pass at the end keeps the reported
/// order the same as the order the walk found them in.
export fn graft(head: Option<Mutation>, tail: Option<Mutation>) -> Option<Mutation>:
    var acc: Option<Mutation> = tail; var cur = head; var cont = true
    while cont:
        match cur:
            Some(m):
                acc = Some(mutation(m.kind, m.parent_uid, m.index, m.uid, m.detail, acc))
                cur = m.next
            None:
                cont = false
    acc

/// INTERNAL — [`graft`] with the roles swapped, which is how a
/// per-child result is folded into the running chain.
export fn join(head: Option<Mutation>, tail: Option<Mutation>) -> Option<Mutation>:
    if tail_is_empty(tail):
        head
    else:
        graft(head, tail)

/// INTERNAL — whether a chain has nothing in it. A named helper because
/// matching an `Option` just to learn "empty" is a five-line block that
/// would otherwise be repeated at every join.
export fn tail_is_empty(t: Option<Mutation>) -> Bool:
    match t:
        Some(_): false
        None: true

/// INTERNAL — a chain with `m` in front of `rest`.
export fn push(head: Option<Mutation>, m: Mutation) -> Option<Mutation>:
    Some(mutation(m.kind, m.parent_uid, m.index, m.uid, m.detail, head))

/// INTERNAL — the chain reversed. The walk prepends, because a prepend
/// is the only chain operation that does not copy, and a report reads
/// far better in discovery order.
export fn reverse(head: Option<Mutation>) -> Option<Mutation>:
    var out: Option<Mutation> = None; var cur = head; var cont = true
    while cont:
        match cur:
            Some(m):
                out = Some(mutation(m.kind, m.parent_uid, m.index, m.uid, m.detail, out))
                cur = m.next
            None:
                cont = false
    out

/// How many changes the diff found. Exact: the chain is unbounded, so
/// unlike a fixed-capacity accumulator there is nothing to overflow.
export fn diff_count(d: Diff) -> Int:
    d.n

/// The change at `i` in report order, or `None` when `i` is out of
/// range.
export fn mutation_at(d: Diff, i: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    var out: Option<Mutation> = None; var cur = d.head; var k = 0; var cont = true
    while cont:
        match cur:
            Some(m):
                if k == i:
                    out = Some(m); cont = false
                else:
                    k = k + 1; cur = m.next
            None:
                cont = false
    out

/// The kind of a change, for a caller that matches on it rather than
/// printing [`describe_mutation`].
export fn mutation_kind(m: Mutation) -> MutationKind:
    m.kind

/// The kind's name, as written in the source, so a report and a test
/// agree on the spelling.
export fn mutation_name(k: MutationKind) -> String:
    if k == ReplaceNode:
        "ReplaceNode"
    else:
        if k == Reidentify:
            "Reidentify"
        else:
            if k == Retitle:
                "Retitle"
            else:
                if k == Enable:
                    "Enable"
                else:
                    if k == Disable:
                        "Disable"
                    else:
                        if k == Repad:
                            "Repad"
                        else:
                            if k == Realign:
                                "Realign"
                            else:
                                if k == InsertChild:
                                    "InsertChild"
                                else:
                                    if k == RemoveChild:
                                        "RemoveChild"
                                    else:
                                        "MoveChild"

/// One change as a line, e.g.
/// `MoveChild uid=7 2->0 (under uid=1)`.
export fn describe_mutation(m: Mutation) -> String:
    "{mutation_name(m.kind)} {detail(m)}"

/// INTERNAL — the locating half of [`describe_mutation`]: which uid, at
/// which index, under which parent. Empty for a change with nothing to
/// locate, rather than a line of zeroes.
export fn detail(m: Mutation) -> String:
    if m.parent_uid == 0:
        "uid={m.uid} at {m.index}"
    else:
        "uid={m.uid} at {m.index} (under uid={m.parent_uid})"

/// The whole diff as text, one change per line, prefixed with a count.
/// `"(no changes)"` when the two trees are the same.
export fn describe(d: Diff) -> String:
    if d.n == 0:
        "(no changes)"
    else:
        let first: Option<Mutation> = d.head
        match first:
            Some(m): describe_from(m, d.n)
            None: "(no changes)"

/// INTERNAL — [`describe`]'s walk, with the count in the header so a
/// caller sees "3 changes" and the three lines together.
export fn describe_from(m: Mutation, n: Int) -> String:
    var out = "{n} changes:"; var cur: Option<Mutation> = Some(m); var cont = true
    while cont:
        match cur:
            Some(x):
                out = out + "\n" + describe_mutation(x); cur = x.next
            None:
                cont = false
    out

/// The larger of two lengths — the loop bound when pairing two child
/// lists of possibly different lengths.
export fn bigger(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int:
    if a > b:
        a
    else:
        b

/// The index of the child with `uid`, or `-1`.
/// 
/// `-1` and not `None` because the answer feeds an `if` that decides
/// whether a uid moved, and `-1` is the shape the rest of this module's
/// index arithmetic already uses.
export fn index_of_uid(cs: [Arc<Widget>], id: Int) -> Int:
    var out = 0 - 1; var i = 0; var cont = true
    while cont:
        if i < cs.length:
            if arc_load_builtin(cs[i]).uid == id:
                out = i; cont = false
            else:
                i = i + 1
        else:
            cont = false
    out

/// Whether no two of `cs` share a non-zero `uid`.
/// 
/// Sibling-scoped on purpose: "a child has exactly one parent" is a
/// statement about a sibling list, and checking it there needs no set.
/// A uid that appears in two *different* subtrees is legal and is not
/// rejected — a uid is an identity a caller chooses, and reusing one
/// across branches is the caller's business.
export fn uids_unique(cs: [Arc<Widget>]) -> Bool:
    var ok = true; var i = 0
    while i < cs.length:
        let a: Widget = arc_load_builtin(cs[i])
        if a.uid != 0:
            var j = i + 1
            while j < cs.length:
                if arc_load_builtin(cs[j]).uid == a.uid:
                    ok = false
                j = j + 1
        i = i + 1
    ok

/// INTERNAL — one node's own shape and depth, given that it is the child
/// of a node at `parent_depth`.
/// 
/// The three failures it can report, in the order it looks for them:
/// a child's `uid` that is not unique among its siblings; a child whose
/// stamped `depth` is not `parent_depth + 1`; and a kind whose arity its
/// own node violates. On success it hands back the node's children, so
/// the caller can push them without touching the node again.
export fn check_child(w: Widget, parent_depth: Int) -> Result<[Arc<Widget>], String>:
    let cs: [Arc<Widget>] = w.children
    if uids_unique(cs) == false:
        Err("sibling uids are not unique under a {widget.kind_of(w)}")
    else:
        if w.depth != parent_depth + 1:
            Err("{widget.kind_of(w)} at depth {w.depth}, expected {parent_depth + 1}")
        else:
            match check_shape(w, cs):
                Ok(_): Ok(cs)
                Err(problem): Err(problem)

/// INTERNAL — a node's arity against its kind. Split out of
/// [`check_child`] so the depth and uid checks and the shape check read
/// as two separate statements about two separate invariants.
export fn check_shape(w: Widget, cs: [Arc<Widget>]) -> Result<Widget, String>:
    if widget.is_leaf(w.kind):
        if cs.length > 0:
            Err("a {widget.kind_of(w)} is a leaf but has {cs.length} children")
        else:
            Ok(w)
    else:
        if widget.is_single(w.kind):
            if cs.length != 1:
                Err("a {widget.kind_of(w)} takes one child, found {cs.length}")
            else:
                Ok(w)
        else:
            Ok(w)

/// INTERNAL — push every child of `w` onto a worklist, so each is
/// checked with `w`'s depth as its parent depth.
export fn schedule(todo: Option<Pending>, cs: [Arc<Widget>]) -> Option<Pending>:
    var out = todo; var i = 0
    while i < cs.length:
        let c: Widget = arc_load_builtin(cs[i]); out = Some(Pending { node: c, next: out }); i = i + 1
    out

/// The whole-tree check. `Ok(root)` when the tree is well formed, and
/// the **first** problem as `Err(String)` when it is not.
/// 
/// First-error rather than a list, for the same reason every other
/// first-party library in this repo answers with one error: the language
/// cannot accumulate a list of them, and a caller that gets one concrete
/// message can act on it. The three checks are tried root-first, so the
/// error names the outermost problem rather than a symptom of it.
/// 
/// Iterative on purpose — see [`Pending`].
export fn validate_tree(root: Widget) -> Result<Widget, String>:
    if root.depth != 0:
        Err("a root must be at depth 0, found {root.depth}")
    else:
        match check_root(root):
            Ok(_): Ok(root)
            Err(problem): Err(problem)

/// INTERNAL — [`validate_tree`]'s walk: seed the worklist with the root
/// and drain it, pushing each node's children as it goes.
export fn check_root(root: Widget) -> Result<Widget, String>:
    var todo: Option<Pending> = Some(Pending { node: root, next: None }); var bad = false
    var problem = ""; var cont = true
    while cont:
        match todo:
            Some(p):
                todo = p.next
                match check_one(p.node):
                    Ok(cs):
                        todo = schedule(todo, cs)
                    Err(why):
                        bad = true; problem = why; cont = false
            None:
                cont = false
    if bad:
        Err(problem)
    else:
        Ok(root)

/// INTERNAL — one node against the tree's rules. A node with no parent
/// (the root) is checked for shape only; anything else is checked as
/// somebody's child, so a hand-built literal with a wrong `depth` or a
/// duplicated `uid` is caught.
export fn check_one(w: Widget) -> Result<[Arc<Widget>], String>:
    if w.depth == 0:
        match check_shape(w, w.children):
            Ok(_): Ok(w.children)
            Err(why): Err(why)
    else:
        check_child(w, w.depth - 1)

/// Build the tree and refuse to hand back a malformed one.
/// 
/// The original `build_tree(root) -> Widget` validated nothing and
/// returned its argument; the useful version of that idea is a `Result`,
/// so a caller writes `let root = build_tree(candidate)?` at the top of a
/// `build` and cannot forget the check. On success the value is the
/// argument, unchanged: validation does not repair a tree, it refuses
/// one.
export fn build_tree(root: Widget) -> Result<Widget, String>:
    validate_tree(root)

/// INTERNAL — the `ReplaceNode` a kind change produces. A replaced node
/// is not descended into: its whole subtree is new, and reporting the
/// children of a node that is going away as individual mutations would be
/// noise.
export fn replace_delta(old: Widget, next: Widget, parent_uid: Int, index: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    Some(
    lone(
    ReplaceNode,
    parent_uid,
    index,
    next.uid,
    "kind {widget.kind_of(old)} -> {widget.kind_of(next)}",
),
)

/// INTERNAL — the `Reidentify` a `uid` change produces, and only when
/// both sides actually carry one: two nodes both at `uid` 0 are
/// "unidentified", which is the same state, not a change of identity.
export fn id_delta(old: Widget, next: Widget, parent_uid: Int, index: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    if old.uid == next.uid || old.uid == 0 && next.uid == 0:
        None
    else:
        Some(lone(Reidentify, parent_uid, index, next.uid, "uid {old.uid} -> {next.uid}"))

/// INTERNAL — the `Retitle` a text change produces.
export fn text_delta(old: Widget, next: Widget, parent_uid: Int, index: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    if old.text == next.text:
        None
    else:
        Some(lone(Retitle, parent_uid, index, next.uid, "\"{old.text}\" -> \"{next.text}\""))

/// INTERNAL — the `Enable` / `Disable` an `enabled` change produces. Two
/// separate variants rather than one with a boolean, so a report reads as
/// a fact instead of a value to be interpreted.
export fn enabled_delta(old: Widget, next: Widget, parent_uid: Int, index: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    if old.enabled == next.enabled:
        None
    else:
        if next.enabled:
            Some(lone(Enable, parent_uid, index, next.uid, "enabled"))
        else:
            Some(lone(Disable, parent_uid, index, next.uid, "disabled"))

/// INTERNAL — the `Repad` an insets change produces. The four sides are
/// in the message because "the padding changed" is not an actionable
/// report.
export fn pad_delta(old: Widget, next: Widget, parent_uid: Int, index: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    if widget.same_insets(old.padding, next.padding):
        None
    else:
        let a = widget.insets_of(old); let b = widget.insets_of(next)
        Some(
    lone(
    Repad,
    parent_uid,
    index,
    next.uid,
    "{a.left},{a.top},{a.right},{a.bottom} -> {b.left},{b.top},{b.right},{b.bottom}",
),
)

/// INTERNAL — the `Realign` an alignment change produces. The two
/// tenths are in the message, because "the alignment changed" is not an
/// actionable report.
export fn align_delta(old: Widget, next: Widget, parent_uid: Int, index: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    if widget.same_align(old.alignment, next.alignment):
        None
    else:
        let a = widget.alignment_of(old); let b = widget.alignment_of(next)
        Some(lone(Realign, parent_uid, index, next.uid, "{a.x},{a.y} -> {b.x},{b.y}"))

/// INTERNAL — every change to one node's own properties, in a fixed
/// order: identity, then text, then enabled, then the two box
/// properties. A fixed order is what makes the diff output byte-stable
/// and therefore assertable.
export fn node_delta(old: Widget, next: Widget, parent_uid: Int, index: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    if old.kind != next.kind:
        replace_delta(old, next, parent_uid, index)
    else:
        let a = id_delta(old, next, parent_uid, index)
        let b = text_delta(old, next, parent_uid, index)
        let c = enabled_delta(old, next, parent_uid, index)
        let d = pad_delta(old, next, parent_uid, index)
        let e = align_delta(old, next, parent_uid, index); join(join(join(join(a, b), c), d), e)

/// INTERNAL — one child slot of two parents.
/// 
/// The three cases: both sides have a child there, so pair them; only the
/// new side does, so it is an insertion; only the old side does, so it is
/// a removal. A pair is handed to [`diff_pair`] unless the two children's
/// `uid`s disagree *and* the old one turns up elsewhere in the new list,
/// which is a move rather than a replace.
export fn diff_slot(oc: [Arc<Widget>], nc: [Arc<Widget>], i: Int, parent_uid: Int, depth: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    if i < oc.length && i < nc.length:
        let a: Widget = arc_load_builtin(oc[i]); let b: Widget = arc_load_builtin(nc[i])
        if a.uid != 0 && a.uid != b.uid:
            let at = index_of_uid(nc, a.uid)
            if at < 0:
                diff_pair(a, b, parent_uid, i, depth + 1)
            else:
                let moved = lone(MoveChild, parent_uid, at, a.uid, "moved to {at}")
                join(Some(moved), diff_pair(a, arc_load_builtin(nc[at]), parent_uid, at, depth + 1))
        else:
            diff_pair(a, b, parent_uid, i, depth + 1)
    else:
        if i < nc.length:
            Some(lone(InsertChild, parent_uid, i, arc_load_builtin(nc[i]).uid, "inserted at {i}"))
        else:
            Some(lone(RemoveChild, parent_uid, i, arc_load_builtin(oc[i]).uid, "removed from {i}"))

/// INTERNAL — one paired node: its own changes, then its children's.
/// 
/// `parent_uid` and `index` are the pair's own location, and are passed
/// down so every mutation in the subtree can say where it happened
/// without threading a path.
export fn diff_pair(a: Widget, b: Widget, parent_uid: Int, index: Int, depth: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    let own = node_delta(a, b, parent_uid, index)
    if depth >= diff_max_depth():
        own
    else:
        let kids = diff_kids(a, b, parent_uid, depth); join(own, kids)

/// INTERNAL — the child slots of a pair, walked to the longer of the two
/// lists. Returns nothing at all once the depth bound is reached, which
/// is what bounds the recursion.
export fn diff_kids(a: Widget, b: Widget, parent_uid: Int, depth: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    if depth >= diff_max_depth():
        None
    else:
        let oc: [Arc<Widget>] = a.children; let nc: [Arc<Widget>] = b.children
        var out: Option<Mutation> = None; var i = 0; let stop = bigger(oc.length, nc.length)
        while i < stop:
            out = join(out, diff_slot(oc, nc, i, parent_uid, depth)); i = i + 1
        out

/// Compare two trees by HEAP IDENTITY, descending only where the
/// objects actually differ.
///
/// This is the re-render path, and it sits *alongside* `diff_pair`
/// rather than replacing it: `diff_pair` walks values and stays the
/// pure-value reference (and its tests stay green untouched), while this
/// walks `Arc`s and prunes. The two must agree structurally — see
/// `libs/ui/tests/rerender_test.nv`, which asserts the same trees
/// produce the same mutation count on both paths.
///
/// Pruning rule, and its limit stated plainly: same heap object
/// (`arc_id` equal) means nothing below can differ, so return `None`
/// without loading a single value. That is the O(1) fast path. There is
/// deliberately NO prune on `version` equality: versions track structure
/// (depth/count), not content, so two different texts at the same
/// position carry the same version and version-pruning would SILENTLY
/// MISS a real change. A version that pruned safely would have to fold
/// content in (a hash), which is a bigger design; until then, differing
/// identities always descend. The cost model is therefore O(positions)
/// cheap comparisons plus O(changed) value work — not O(changed) alone,
/// and the benchmark asserts exactly that.
export managed fn diff_live_pair(a: Arc<Widget>, b: Arc<Widget>, parent_uid: Int, index: Int, depth: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    if arc_id_builtin(a) == arc_id_builtin(b):
        None
    else:
        let old = arc_load_builtin(a)
        let next = arc_load_builtin(b)
        let own = node_delta(old, next, parent_uid, index)
        if depth >= diff_max_depth():
            own
        else:
            let kids = diff_live_kids(a, b, parent_uid, depth); join(own, kids)

/// The child slots of a live pair, walked to the longer of the two
/// lists. Takes the `Arc`s (not loaded values) so each slot can prune
/// on identity before anything is loaded. Same walk order and same
/// `Mutation` shapes as `diff_kids` — only the pruning is new.
export managed fn diff_live_kids(a: Arc<Widget>, b: Arc<Widget>, parent_uid: Int, depth: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    if depth >= diff_max_depth():
        None
    else:
        let oc: [Arc<Widget>] = arc_load_builtin(a).children
        let nc: [Arc<Widget>] = arc_load_builtin(b).children
        var out: Option<Mutation> = None; var i = 0; let stop = bigger(oc.length, nc.length)
        while i < stop:
            out = join(out, diff_live_slot(oc, nc, i, parent_uid, depth)); i = i + 1
        out

/// One child slot of two live parents. Mirrors `diff_slot` exactly —
/// same uid-move detection, same insert/remove shapes — except the pair
/// it recurses into is the live (pruning) one.
export managed fn diff_live_slot(oc: [Arc<Widget>], nc: [Arc<Widget>], i: Int, parent_uid: Int, depth: Int) -> Option<Mutation>:
    if i < oc.length && i < nc.length:
        // Identity first: a shared child Arc means the whole subtree is
        // unchanged, so skip the two value loads entirely. This is what
        // keeps a mostly-shared frame cheap — loading is what copies, and
        // copies are what made the value diff superlinear.
        if arc_id_builtin(oc[i]) == arc_id_builtin(nc[i]):
            None
        else:
            let a: Widget = arc_load_builtin(oc[i]); let b: Widget = arc_load_builtin(nc[i])
            if a.uid != 0 && a.uid != b.uid:
                let at = index_of_uid(nc, a.uid)
                if at < 0:
                    diff_live_pair(oc[i], nc[i], parent_uid, i, depth + 1)
                else:
                    let moved = lone(MoveChild, parent_uid, at, a.uid, "moved to {at}")
                    join(Some(moved), diff_live_pair(oc[i], nc[at], parent_uid, at, depth + 1))
            else:
                diff_live_pair(oc[i], nc[i], parent_uid, i, depth + 1)
    else:
        if i < nc.length:
            Some(lone(InsertChild, parent_uid, i, arc_load_builtin(nc[i]).uid, "inserted at {i}"))
        else:
            Some(lone(RemoveChild, parent_uid, i, arc_load_builtin(oc[i]).uid, "removed from {i}"))

/// Compare two trees with the default depth bound. Changes come back in
/// walk order.
/// 
/// A tree that fails [`validate_tree`] is still diffable — the diff does
/// not care about the ownership stamp, only about the values — so the
/// two checks are independent and a caller may run either, both or
/// neither.
export fn diff_widgets(old: Widget, next: Widget) -> Diff:
    diff_widgets_depth(old, next, diff_max_depth())

/// Compare two trees, descending at most `max_depth` levels.
/// 
/// Past the bound a paired node's own properties are still compared and
/// its children are not, so a caller that needs the whole answer for a
/// deep tree has to raise this. Exposed rather than hidden because the
/// bound is a property of the runtime's call-stack budget, not of the
/// diff.
export fn diff_widgets_depth(old: Widget, next: Widget, max_depth: Int) -> Diff:
    let found = diff_pair(old, next, 0, 0 - 1, 0)
    Diff { n: chain_length(found), head: reverse(found) }

/// INTERNAL — how long a mutation chain is. A walk, because the count
/// has to be exact and the chain has no length field to keep in step.
export fn chain_length(head: Option<Mutation>) -> Int:
    var n = 0; var cur = head; var cont = true
    while cont:
        match cur:
            Some(m):
                n = n + 1; cur = m.next
            None:
                cont = false
    n

/// How many of the diff's changes are of kind `k` — "how many labels
/// changed?", which is the question a test and a report both ask.
export fn count_of(d: Diff, k: MutationKind) -> Int:
    var n = 0; var cur = d.head; var cont = true
    while cont:
        match cur:
            Some(m):
                if m.kind == k:
                    n = n + 1
                cur = m.next
            None:
                cont = false
    n

/// Whether the diff found a change of kind `k`.
export fn has_kind(d: Diff, k: MutationKind) -> Bool:
    count_of(d, k) > 0
